4. Published sources: values and conventions cited
--------------------------------------------------

- Drillis, R.; Contini, R. (1966). Body Segment Parameters. New York University, School of
  Engineering and Science, Technical Report 1166.03. Reproduced in Winter, D.A. (2009),
  Biomechanics and Motor Control of Human Movement, 4th ed., Wiley, Fig. 4.1.
  Joint-height ratios of the canonical skeleton.
- Trotter, M.; Gleser, G.C. (1952). Estimation of stature from long bones of American Whites
  and Negroes. doi:10.1002/ajpa.1330100407.
- Trotter, M. (1970). Estimation of stature from intact long limb bones.
- Wu, G. et al. (2002). ISB recommendation on definitions of joint coordinate systems, part I.
  J Biomech 35:543-548. Geometry convention only.
- Wu, G. et al. (2005). ISB recommendation on definitions of joint coordinate systems, part II
  (shoulder). J Biomech 38:981-992. Landmark convention only.

The complete list, with what each source supports, is in
src/character_engine/data/anatomy/references/source_registry.json.


5. Used outside the repository: nothing committed
-------------------------------------------------

Kept in a local folder and not redistributed here:

- human-atlas (https://github.com/ashemag/human-atlas): code MIT, data CC BY 4.0, itself
  derived from BodyParts3D. Mesh-to-system classification used as a working aid.
- Quaternius Universal Animation Library: CC0. Test clips; only local paths are recorded,
  in scripts/batch_specs/.
- Judo manuals and the owner's video playlist, and the anatomy textbook Anatomía Clínica (Pró):
  all rights reserved. Consulted as references; cited, never copied.


